course: dictionary
lesson: 15685
title: A word for clocks and watches
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A timepiece is a slightly old or formal word for a clock or a watch. You will not hear it in everyday talk very often, but you will meet it in stories and on shop adverts.
explain: «The White Rabbit pulled a watch out of his waistcoat pocket and hurried on.» The clues are watch and pocket, so a timepiece is something you carry and check to see the time.
explain: «The shop sold a fine timepiece with a golden face.» Fine means lovely or high quality. To check your meaning, swap in clock or watch: the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
fact: Timepiece means a clock or a watch.
fact: Any instrument for measuring time can be called a timepiece.
fact: Timepiece is a slightly old or formal word, so you will meet it more in books and adverts than in everyday talk.
fact: Early timepieces include sundials, hourglasses and water clocks.
fact: In «Alice in Wonderland», the Mad Hatter's timepiece tells the day of the month, not the hour.
exercise: «The old shop sold a fine timepiece with a golden face.» What is a timepiece? | A shop window | A kind of hat | *A clock or watch | A small garden tool
exercise: Which of these is an early timepiece? | A bicycle | A pair of gloves | A microphone | *A sundial
exercise: Find and copy the one word that tells you where the White Rabbit kept his watch: «The White Rabbit pulled a watch out of his waistcoat pocket.» | watch | *pocket | rabbit | pulled
test: Ella says a timepiece is a sort of cake. Who is right? | Ella's dad | *Nobody, a timepiece is a clock or watch | Both of them are right | Ella
test: Which of these is a water clock, an early timepiece? | A bowl of soup | A glass of juice | A paper cup | *A container of slowly dripping water used to measure time
exam: What does timepiece mean? | *Any clock or watch, or instrument for measuring time | A measuring cup for flour | A song sung at school | A kind of pet
